<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Fabricator.SamplePlugin.Support</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Fabricator.SamplePlugin.Support</AssemblyName>
    <Description>
      A PRIVATE DEPENDENCY of Fabricator.SamplePlugin, and a test fixture with exactly one job: to be an
      assembly the plugin needs, the HOST does not ship, and the loader therefore has to find beside the
      plugin.

      ⚠ WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL. The failure it guards is measured, not imagined: a LIBRARY does not copy its
      dependency closure to the output directory unless CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies is set, and when
      Fabricator.FluidPlugin was created the first build produced one DLL and nothing else — it loaded, and
      died at the first render with a FileNotFoundException naming an assembly that was never copied. Fluid
      was the only in-tree plugin with a private dependency, so when it became a built-in provider assembly
      (2026-09-02) that coverage would have gone to zero: Fabricator.SamplePlugin is pure IL, and
      pack-distribution's "a bundled plugin must have more than one assembly" assertion went with the
      bundling.

      ⚠ A SIBLING PROJECT RATHER THAN A NuGet PACKAGE, deliberately. A third-party package would add a
      supply-chain surface to a test asset and a second version to keep aligned with the host's (the
      Apache.Arrow hazard), and it would have to be one the host does NOT already ship — otherwise the
      plugin scan skips it by simple name as `shared` and the fixture proves nothing.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
